## Why This Is Asked

Streaming APIs combined with REST surfaces are common in large-scale systems. This question probes testing of delivery guarantees, ordering, resilience under partial outages, and observability—areas critical for Snap, Microsoft, and Google-scale services.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end testing across REST and WebSocket channels
- Message ordering and sequencing guarantees
- Delivery semantics (at-least-once vs exactly-once) and replay safety
- Reconnection, backoff, and backfill strategies using durable offsets
- Handling partial upstream outages and dynamic schema changes
- Observability with tracing, logs, and metrics

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: basic WebSocket stream ordering test (conceptual)
const WebSocket = require('ws');
const ws = new WebSocket('wss://example.com/stream');
let lastSeq = -1;
let ok = true;
let count = 0;
ws.on('message', (data) => {
  const msg = JSON.parse(data);
  if (typeof msg.seq !== 'number' || msg.seq <= lastSeq) ok = false;
  lastSeq = msg.seq;
  count++;
  if (count >= 100) ws.close();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate network partitions and validate reconnect/backoff behavior across regions?
- How would you validate exactly-once semantics when an event is replayed during backfill?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests your ability to design comprehensive testing strategies for distributed systems. Multi-region ride-hailing APIs involve complex interactions between services, making end-to-end testing critical for reliability and user experience.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end testing across microservices
- Latency SLIs, error budgets, and SLOs
- Circuit breakers and exponential backoff retries
- Chaos testing and graceful degradation strategies
- Contract testing with OpenAPI specifications and service mocks
- Cross-region data consistency and eventual consistency patterns

## Code Example
```javascript
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check, sleep } from 'k6';

export let options = { 
  vus: 100, 
  duration: '30s',
  thresholds: {
    http_req_duration: ['p(95)<500'],
    http_req_failed: ['rate<0.01']
  }
};

export default function() {
  let payload = {
    rider_id: 'user_123',
    pickup: { lat: 40.7128, lng: -74.0060 },
    destination: { lat: 40.7589, lng: -73.9851 }
  };

  let r = http.post('https://rides.example.com/v1/rides', 
    JSON.stringify(payload), 
    { headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/json'} }
  );

  check(r, {
    'status is 201': (r) => r.status === 201,
    'response time < 500ms': (r) => r.timings.duration < 500,
    'ride ID returned': (r) => JSON.parse(r.body).ride_id
  });

  sleep(1);
}
```
